  "answer": "Yes—other elements can neutralise or diminish a problematic connotation, but this is not automatic.\n\nManual practice: consider the trade mark as a whole and how the relevant consumer would interpret it. Other features can diminish the impact of offending material. The Manual illustrates this with the Bavaria decision, quoting the finding that three references to “Holland” at least neutralised the connotation with Bavaria [TMM/Part29/3/3/5]. Its guidance on standalone geographical references also expressly recognises that other elements or surrounding circumstances may mitigate the risk of confusion [TMM/Part29/9/9/3~1].\n\nAct: section 43 requires rejection where, because of a connotation of the mark or a sign within it, use for the relevant goods or services would be likely to deceive or cause confusion. Thus, the statutory question remains whether that likelihood exists after considering the mitigating elements—not merely whether a potentially problematic element is present [TMA1995/s43; TMM/Part29/3/3/5]."
